WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.950
- All right, so you
like me, might have seen

00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:04.560
a lot of Formula 1 recently,

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.750
a lot of races, a lot of famous drivers,

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:08.850
a lot of drama and exciting stories.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:11.580
It's taken over my timeline
on Twitter many times.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:12.990
Basically, a ton of you guys
have been saying it to me,

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:14.370
asking, "Hey, are you watching?

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.050
Are you paying attention to this stuff?"

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:19.890
And so I finally caved and
I've started watching it

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:20.970
and I'm really glad I did.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.040
So in this video, I'm gonna tell you guys

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.640
why I think Formula 1 is super
cool, why I started watching,

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.770
and why you should probably
be watching Formula 1 too.

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:32.790
From the stories to the
tech, to the innovation,

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.683
to the race-day experiences.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:36.713
This is Formula 1 explained.

00:00:38.555 --> 00:00:41.250
(mellow music)

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:42.930
So shout out to sponsor Bitdefender

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:44.400
for making this video possible.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.250
They reached out because they
are a Ferrari team partner,

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.770
so they're on the Ferrari
car and the helmet

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.750
and they have a lot of
parallels in the tech world

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:53.220
with cybersecurity and safety

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:54.510
so we'll talk more about that in a minute.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:57.540
But for now, I'm so glad that
y'all put this on my radar

00:00:57.540 --> 00:00:59.370
because I love it now,

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:03.120
just because while on the
surface it is just a car race,

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.810
which is already pretty cool,
it is also, at the same time,

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:10.890
the world's highest-end tech competition.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:11.723
It's a science fair.

00:01:11.723 --> 00:01:13.110
So at the most basic level,

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:15.990
the sport is a year-long
season of car races

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:17.670
on different tracks around the world.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.770
And Formula 1 has a long and rich history.

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.510
I don't need to give you a
whole history lesson here,

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:24.390
but there's been a
massive amount of changes

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:26.280
going from stuff like this,

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:29.700
like a pretty simple looking
tube with an engine and wheels,

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:33.060
to this aerodynamic work of art

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.400
made from the most advanced
materials on the planet.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.060
But the most interesting
part about F1 that I love

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.790
is that there are
actually two championships

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:43.920
at the same time, each season.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.810
There's the Drivers' Championship

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.330
and the Constructors' Championship.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:49.980
So yes, every driver is gonna try

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:51.810
to get around the track
as fast as possible,

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.190
but the structure is:

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:55.980
there are 10 teams, each
team has two active drivers.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.380
And so every race, a driver can get points

00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.640
for how high they finish.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.920
Those add up at the end of
the season to get a winner.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.330
But there's also points

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:06.210
for the engineering teams behind each car.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.580
And they also get points that add up

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.200
to a championship at the end of the year.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.120
And this, this is what I mean

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.860
when I say it's a science fair.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:16.710
'Cause look, every sport has rules, right?

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.830
Like golf, all your clubs need
to be within a certain spec

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:22.860
and the basketball hoop
is standardized, right?

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.770
But having the best equipment in F1

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:28.830
is incredibly advantageous,
as you might suspect,

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:31.530
and you're allowed,
actually, encouraged even,

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:35.400
to improve your equipment
between every single race.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.500
You are constantly
working on your equipment.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.850
In so many ways

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:42.360
F1 is the sport with
the most tech involved.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.360
Like these teams are spending
millions and millions,

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.140
hundreds of millions of
dollars to find/invent

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.540
any little improvement
that they can come up with

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:54.540
to give themselves the
edge every single week.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.780
They're fighting for tenths
and hundredths of a second.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:58.860
It's incredible to see.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.020
Like, you don't get from here

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.360
to here so quickly by accident.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:06.270
There is a massive amount of engineering

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:08.340
and experimenting that
goes into optimizing

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:11.100
every single little part of every car

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.600
to be able to get around the track

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.760
as fast as physically possible,

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.800
to get as many points for the championship

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.170
every season within the rules.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:20.730
And yes, there are still rules.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:21.563
This is important.

00:03:21.563 --> 00:03:24.900
So the FIA is the governing
body in charge of F1

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.750
and they are charged
with the impossible task

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.160
of maintaining a set of rules

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.890
that keeps everything fair and safe,

00:03:31.890 --> 00:03:33.930
but still allows room for

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:37.260
and encourages innovation
and tweaking things.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.240
So you can actually look
up the rule book yourself.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.820
There's a 183-page PDF online.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:42.870
I'll leave a link below the like button

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:44.100
if you wanna flip through it yourself.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.020
And there are so many
interesting rules in here,

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.600
some of them to keep racing
strategies interesting,

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.820
like the requirement
that every single driver

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.700
uses at least two different
tire compounds during the race.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:54.690
I'll explain that in a minute.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:56.490
There are also plenty
of safety-related rules,

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:58.440
which, again, is why a
company like Bitdefender

00:03:58.440 --> 00:03:59.640
really makes sense to be here.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:02.550
So these are open-wheel,
open-cockpit race cars,

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.710
but every single one
of them, as you'll see,

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:08.250
no matter what, has this
iron-reinforced ring

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:11.640
around and above the
driver's head called a halo.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.230
This thing is not designed by the teams.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:15.750
This is designed by the
FIA and the teams get them

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:17.310
from one of the approved manufacturers

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:19.200
and they have to put them on their car.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:20.520
There was initially some pushback

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:21.510
against the aesthetics of it,

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:24.870
but every single year since
its introduction in 2018,

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.870
it's continued to prove its
worth in the safety department.

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:29.850
Like, you can literally
go watch race footage

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:32.550
of incidents where the halo saves lives

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.350
since it can literally support the weight

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:36.660
of another entire car on top of you,

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.550
or if it gets flipped upside down.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.020
So you can bet that's why Bitdefender

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:43.200
wanted their logo specifically on the halo

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.820
of the Team Ferrari car.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:46.710
So Bitdefender is a
leader in cybersecurity,

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:49.440
which means they value innovation, safety,

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.830
and quick reactions, which
is tied right into F1.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.020
So you can see why it makes
sense for Bitdefender's logo

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:58.110
to be on the most important
safety features of the cars.

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.030
They're also on the
helmet too, by the way.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:01.050
There's some other high-level rules

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:03.930
around the turbocharged
V6 engines they all use

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:05.760
and the gear boxes, et cetera.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:09.330
My personal favorite
and funniest rule to me

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:10.620
is also one of the oldest,

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:14.130
which is under every single F1 car

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:16.320
is a plank of wood bolted to the bottom

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.110
because one of the OG rules is to restrict

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:22.380
just how low the car can
ride, another safety thing.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:25.590
And so after every race they can inspect

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:27.570
the plank of wood on
the bottom of your car

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:29.820
and if it's too worn down,

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:31.920
then your car was dragging
too low to the ground

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.000
and you have too much down force

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.040
and your car is illegal, disqualified.

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:35.873
So just think about that.

00:05:35.873 --> 00:05:38.340
Next time you see one
of those jet-looking,

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:41.370
marvel-of-engineering
carbon fiber masterpieces

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:42.390
flying around the racetrack,

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:44.160
just know that every single one of them

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:47.100
also has a plank of wood
bolted to the bottom of it.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.830
So you've got this rule book,
everybody knows the rules,

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:52.200
and then you've got all these
teams independently working,

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.170
spending millions of dollars
to try to find any experiment,

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.810
any little thing that might give their car

00:05:57.810 --> 00:05:59.880
the advantage around the track.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:01.860
And naturally, since it's so competitive,

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.110
these teams are incredibly secretive

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:05.280
about everything they're doing.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:06.780
But then, the beautiful thing is

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:09.210
if some team actually
comes up with something

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:12.420
that's good enough to give
them a real advantage,

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:15.600
even if it's a tenth of a
second, a quarter of a second,

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.483
a half a second per lap, that's massive,

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:22.440
eventually, all of the other
teams will pick up on it

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:24.210
and will find a way to build it

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:25.620
and implement it themselves.

00:06:25.620 --> 00:06:27.300
It's just this ecosystem

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:28.710
of all these constantly-improving features

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:30.600
and you can see them
stacking on top of each other

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:31.433
over and over again.

00:06:31.433 --> 00:06:33.210
"So what exactly are these improvements?",

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:34.680
you might be wondering.

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.900
Great question. Here are
some of the biggest ones.

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:38.280
And basically every single one of these

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:39.870
has an amazing story behind it

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:42.480
of how someone on an
engineering team had an idea

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:44.130
and then they decided to try it

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:46.050
and then it worked and it stuck.

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:48.120
So one of the most obvious
is the wings, right?

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:51.480
Of course we see wings
everywhere on today's cars,

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:54.330
but believe it or not, F1
cars didn't always have wings.

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:56.790
They just relied on pure
mechanical grip from the tires.

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:58.140
But you know how if you stick your hand

00:06:58.140 --> 00:06:59.670
out the car window and then tilt it,

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:01.830
it'll push your hand up or down.

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:03.510
Same thing with a wing on a car.

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:06.900
It can push parts of the car
up or down into the track

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:09.060
for more grip and faster cornering.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.250
So it started off as a
very small experiment

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:14.250
on the front axle and, very
quickly, it started working

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:16.950
and now they all have
front wings and rear wings.

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:19.230
Fun fact, one team did actually once try

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:22.290
a mini extra front wing on a car in 2001.

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:23.310
It didn't really work out

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:25.530
so you don't really see
that anymore. (chuckles)

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:29.460
The cars today have tons and
tons of aerodynamic features:

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:33.690
multi-layered front wings,
small air-steering little bits,

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:35.400
like fins near the middle of the car.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:37.920
It's crazy how many details there are

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:40.650
if you ever actually get to
look at one of these things.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:44.400
Another huge advancement
is something called KERS,

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.280
an acronym for kinetic
energy recovery system.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:49.290
Basically, a system to capture energy

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:51.000
created by the spinning wheel

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:53.310
and turns it into energy in a battery

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:55.470
that can be deployed as boosts later.

00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:57.750
So the first teams to
try this kind of stuff,

00:07:57.750 --> 00:07:59.670
they actually found it
was a little bit too heavy

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:01.560
and it worked kind of sometimes,

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:03.900
didn't necessarily guarantee a win.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:05.970
But eventually, fast
forward a couple years,

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:07.230
and it's developed over time

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:10.170
and paved the way for
today's hybrid F1 cars.

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:12.900
Now, you might've also
heard of this one, DRS.

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:15.060
This one's pretty simple actually.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:16.590
So yeah, there's a ton of downforce

00:08:16.590 --> 00:08:18.270
from the aerodynamic features of this car

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:20.250
and that helps it stick to the ground

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:21.840
during these planted turns,

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:23.880
but that actually, technically,

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:25.920
slows it down in a straight line.

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:29.340
So teams engineered a
drag reduction system,

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:32.460
a button that you press that
changes the aero of the car,

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:34.770
opens up, lets it cut
through the air more quickly,

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:36.630
and it works really well.

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:39.240
So at this point, not only
do all the cars have it,

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:41.250
but it's actually literally
written into the rules

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:44.280
when you can use it to
maximize racing excitement.

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:45.113
There have been plenty

00:08:45.113 --> 00:08:48.090
of other massive technological
innovations over the years

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:50.640
that have all been pioneered
by just some engineer on a team

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.040
who had a good idea that
have turned out to be huge,

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:55.800
from putting the engine behind the driver,

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:57.660
to the monocoque chassis,

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:00.480
to the ground-effect
aerodynamics, to paddle shifters,

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:01.530
all of them adding up

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:06.530
to absolutely insane
cars of today in 2023.

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:10.950
These cars are clawing for
maximum grip around every corner.

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:14.640
They're hitting 200+ mile
an hour in straightaways

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:19.640
and then pulling five to six
Gs under breaking and turning.

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:24.780
Five to six Gs is about as much

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:27.960
as you can feel anywhere on
earth other than an airplane.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:28.980
So this is why F1

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:31.350
is often called "the
pinnacle of motorsports,"

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:32.220
and I totally agree.

00:09:32.220 --> 00:09:35.220
Like there are technically cars
that are a little bit faster

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:37.320
in a straight line from a standstill.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:38.880
You might see some of
these videos on YouTube.

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:41.970
My car actually is, but the
second you get to a turn,

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:45.090
it gets really obvious
the difference in braking,

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:47.550
grip, acceleration out of a corner,

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:49.080
just the control you have over the car.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:50.880
It's honestly incredible.

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:53.400
And it's one thing to see it on TV,

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:55.140
like you can tell
they're going pretty fast

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:56.970
and the broadcasts are super cool.

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:58.770
There's sensor data everywhere,

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:02.160
live speed readouts, cameras on every car,

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:05.070
but seeing it in-person

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:08.430
gave me an entirely new level
of respect for all of it.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:10.650
So this is the Ferrari car

00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:12.090
the day before racing started

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:13.860
this past weekend in Las Vegas.

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:16.410
This car is so much lower

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:19.260
and actually bigger than
you might expect it to be.

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:23.490
There was also so many little
aerodynamic shapes and pieces

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:26.250
and carbon fiber everywhere,
as I mentioned before.

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:28.440
The tires are also gigantic

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:31.380
and the driver sits so much
lower than you might expect.

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:35.340
Actually, Bitdefender also let
me try a full-on F1 simulator

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:36.990
in their Performance
Zone out here in Vegas.

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:38.970
And the most noticeable thing, actually,

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:40.560
is just how low you sit.

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:42.300
Your feet are kind of back up in the air

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:43.320
right below eye level,

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:45.390
which is where the pedals are in the car.

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:47.280
It's just funny picturing
all the drivers now

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:48.660
in their cars like this.

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:53.490
But what really got me is just
seeing how fast they move.

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:55.770
Cameras just don't do
it justice, I promise.

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:58.140
It's really, really impressive stuff.

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:02.010
And then, there's obviously
the noise and the smells

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:04.200
and the vibration through your chest,

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:06.240
like none of that happens
through the broadcast.

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:08.550
So when Bitdefender reached
out and offered to get me

00:11:08.550 --> 00:11:11.580
to the Las Vegas Grand Prix
to help make this video,

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:13.140
I couldn't say no.

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:16.740
So this is the second-to-last
race of this 2023 F1 season,

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:19.680
a super cool race built
into the city of Las Vegas.

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:20.610
Like one of the straightaways

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:22.830
is literally the Las Vegas strip

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:25.410
and points are available for
the Drivers' Championship

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:27.270
and the Constructors' Championship.

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:31.380
So what is a race day
experience actually like?

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:32.370
Well, here's what to look out for.

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:34.050
So first of all, an F1 Grand Prix

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:36.630
is actually a several-days-long event.

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:40.980
First, there's practice days,
then there's a qualifying day,

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:42.390
then there's the race day.

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:43.980
So the practice day is actually

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:46.080
just a fascinating thing
to watch on its own

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:48.870
because it's a practice
for both the drivers

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:50.880
and the teams engineering the cars.

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:53.130
So there's a set amount of
time that these teams get

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:54.810
to send the drivers out in their cars

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:58.410
to see how much faster or
slower certain setups are.

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:00.240
They all go flying
around the track at once

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:01.440
for the first time

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:03.150
and drivers will literally communicate

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:05.100
with their teams from
a radio in their helmet

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:06.457
about how things are feeling.

00:12:06.457 --> 00:12:08.550
"Oh, you know, a little more
down force here would be nice.

00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:10.380
A little more stiffness
here would be nice."

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:13.560
So they might do a quick
pit stop to swap out,

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:16.230
not just a different set of
tires to see how those feel,

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:18.330
but they might completely
bring it back into the garage

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:20.970
and tweak some of the entire car setup

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:23.070
before they bring it
back out for another lap

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:25.230
and try out some more major adjustments,

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:26.400
all within the rules, of course.

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:27.330
You might have seen the headlines,

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:31.440
this Las Vegas one, the practice
day got a bit interrupted

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:36.240
because one of the cars went
over a manhole cover so fast

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:39.570
that the ground effect
aerodynamic force from the car

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:42.690
sucked the manhole cover out
of the hole in the ground

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:44.370
and damaged the car.

00:12:44.370 --> 00:12:45.480
And so they had to go around

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:48.060
and weld all of the manhole covers shut.

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:48.893
Pretty insane.

00:12:48.893 --> 00:12:50.940
So then the next night is
one more practice session

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:53.010
and then qualifying.

00:12:53.010 --> 00:12:55.200
So qualifying is actually pretty simple.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:58.410
It's go out and set your
absolute fastest lap time

00:12:58.410 --> 00:12:59.640
with your car.

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:01.470
Whoever has the fastest lap time

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:03.720
will start the race in first place

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:05.430
and whoever has the slowest lap time

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:06.480
will start in last place.

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:08.010
So even this gets pretty exciting

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:10.260
because once again,
everyone is out here now

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:13.530
trying to set their qualifying
lap as fast as possible

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:14.910
and they're all trying at once

00:13:14.910 --> 00:13:16.410
so sometimes there's a little traffic

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:17.820
or a little bit of drama,

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:20.250
and there's actually
three qualifying sessions

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:22.020
in a row, elimination style.

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:23.820
So you start with 20 drivers.

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:26.220
The first session
eliminates the slowest five,

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:28.290
the second session, which
is a little shorter,

00:13:28.290 --> 00:13:30.120
eliminates the next slowest five,

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:32.010
and then the third and final session

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:36.000
is the top 10 fastest laps
in order from 1 through 10.

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:36.960
And so everyone is after

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:41.250
that precious first spot on
the grid, aka pole position.

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:42.720
I think the real-time, all the data,

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:43.770
I went down to the garage

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:45.690
when they were starting qualifying,

00:13:45.690 --> 00:13:47.280
those guys are looking at monitors

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:50.550
with everything about the car in real time

00:13:50.550 --> 00:13:54.180
as it's driving on a graph,
being traced on a screen.

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:56.640
It's sick. It's a science fair.

00:13:56.640 --> 00:13:58.350
So there's no screens out here
so you kinda have to follow,

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:00.510
like, on the app, you can
only see the finish line,

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:03.693
but final qualifying,
Ferrari one, Ferrari two.

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:07.020
I didn't plan that, they're just fast.

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:10.170
So then, the third and
final day of the Grand Prix

00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:11.520
is the race.

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:13.830
And a lot of people will not get to go

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:17.730
to an F1 race in person,
but it is really intense.

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:19.470
Like they're all dialed in now.

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:21.390
They're all rolling
out their absolute best

00:14:21.390 --> 00:14:23.760
and final version of
the science fair project

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:25.050
with their best pilots

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:27.000
and they're all gonna now
pit 'em against each other.

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:28.770
So a race is typically about 90 minutes.

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:32.280
It's always 190 miles,
in this case, 50 laps.

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:34.050
And if you're doing the math in your head,

00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:35.820
yes, that does mean they're averaging

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:39.240
well over a hundred miles
an hour for the entire race,

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:40.530
pit stops included.

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:43.050
But then, actually one
key thing to look out for

00:14:43.050 --> 00:14:46.560
while you're watching the
race is pit stop strategy.

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:50.550
So most pit stops during
an F1 race are super quick,

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:53.790
two to four seconds, and
it's mostly just tires.

00:14:53.790 --> 00:14:54.623
Now, they are capable

00:14:54.623 --> 00:14:57.300
of doing some more extensive
repairs during pit stops,

00:14:57.300 --> 00:14:59.520
but those also definitely take longer.

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:01.860
So sometimes if you have like
a damaged wing or something,

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:03.930
they'll just continue on with it

00:15:03.930 --> 00:15:06.360
because it's faster to
not actually replace it.

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:08.603
The winner of the race this
week actually did that.

00:15:08.603 --> 00:15:10.800
And so one of the last biggest rules in F1

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:14.220
that I haven't even mentioned
yet, amazingly, is the tires.

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:16.290
So there's a predefined set of tires

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:19.260
available to all of the
cars that are, again,

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:20.340
not designed by the teams.

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:22.980
They're spec'd by the FIA under the rules

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:24.990
to be exciting for racing.

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:27.090
And basically, there is a set of them

00:15:27.090 --> 00:15:29.940
ranging from softest and grippiest

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:32.970
all the way to hardest and most durable

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:34.200
depending on the rubber compound.

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:37.830
So the softest tires are
gonna be by far the grippiest

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:39.300
and fastest around the track.

00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:42.030
They can be good for a few
seconds faster per lap,

00:15:42.030 --> 00:15:44.130
but believe it or not, they're so soft

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:46.890
that they will wear
down incredibly quickly

00:15:46.890 --> 00:15:49.890
and can be completely
cooked in 10 to 20 laps

00:15:49.890 --> 00:15:51.840
and so you will need to do a pit stop,

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:53.580
which will cost you a little bit of time.

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:54.960
But then, the hardest compounds

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:58.290
are technically slower per lap, less grip,

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:01.410
but they can go way more
laps without a pit stop.

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:03.750
So these drivers are not
only jockeying for position

00:16:03.750 --> 00:16:04.830
to drive their best,

00:16:04.830 --> 00:16:09.000
but teams are also putting
together pit stop strategies

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:12.330
because every car is
required by the rules to use

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:16.110
at least two different tire
compounds during the race,

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:18.900
which produces a lot of
really fascinating scenarios

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:21.450
where one driver is ending the race

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:23.250
with a different set
of tires than another,

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:24.450
depending on their strategy.

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:27.180
It is wild. It's super
interesting to watch.

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:29.820
It's like chess, but the pieces are moving

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:32.460
at 200 miles an hour and
curves and everything.

00:16:32.460 --> 00:16:33.360
It's amazing.

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:34.890
It's another reason why Bitdefender

00:16:34.890 --> 00:16:36.660
aligns itself so well with this stuff

00:16:36.660 --> 00:16:39.030
because again, they're a
leader in cybersecurity.

00:16:39.030 --> 00:16:41.790
In cybersecurity, you have to respond,

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:43.320
you have a strategy to start things,

00:16:43.320 --> 00:16:44.760
but things can go very wrong

00:16:44.760 --> 00:16:47.010
and so they have to be
able to adapt in real time.

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:49.320
And that's a lot of the same
things these drivers are doing,

00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:50.670
that these teams are doing.

00:16:50.670 --> 00:16:52.770
When strategies fall apart,
they gotta tweak things,

00:16:52.770 --> 00:16:55.080
they gotta adjust, they gotta edit.

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:56.280
It's very complicated.

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:59.250
So there's all this
technology, all this strategy,

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:00.720
all of these incredible machines.

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:02.430
But at the end of the day,

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:05.610
you just gotta get out there
and win the race, you know?

00:17:05.610 --> 00:17:08.640
So get in the slipstream, play defense,

00:17:08.640 --> 00:17:10.020
attack braking zones,

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:12.930
use your machine's specific advantages

00:17:12.930 --> 00:17:14.580
to get that rare overtake

00:17:14.580 --> 00:17:17.100
and just all of this being put together,

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:18.300
it's like art on wheels.

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:20.190
There's plenty of YouTube clips out there.

00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:23.250
There's a Netflix documentary
you've probably heard about.

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:24.630
It is amazing to watch.

00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:28.320
And then the cherry on top
for me is all of the effects

00:17:28.320 --> 00:17:32.970
that Formula 1 has on the
world outside of Formula 1.

00:17:32.970 --> 00:17:33.990
It's bigger than you might think.

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:35.550
Like the obvious one is that, okay,

00:17:35.550 --> 00:17:37.890
some of the highest-end,
most amazing innovations

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:40.530
from trying to make the F1 cars faster

00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:42.360
will eventually maybe trickle down

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:44.100
into the road cars that we have today.

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:45.330
And that's true, that's real.

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:47.070
So the Ferrari team
might find something cool

00:17:47.070 --> 00:17:49.440
that works on the racetrack in 2023,

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:51.930
and then in 2029, they're working on

00:17:51.930 --> 00:17:54.000
a simpler, less expensive version,

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:55.740
but that's very effective for a Ferrari

00:17:55.740 --> 00:17:57.090
that someone can actually buy.

00:17:57.090 --> 00:18:00.330
DRS, paddle shifters, carbon fiber,

00:18:00.330 --> 00:18:01.590
there's lots of examples of things

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:03.330
that originated in F1 cars

00:18:03.330 --> 00:18:05.130
that have made their way
to cars that exist today,

00:18:05.130 --> 00:18:05.963
which is super cool.

00:18:05.963 --> 00:18:07.350
But then there's some of
the less obvious things

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:12.350
like a race kind of changes
a whole city forever,

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:13.710
kind of like the Olympics.

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:14.910
Now, a lot of races are on

00:18:14.910 --> 00:18:16.740
dedicated purpose-built racetracks,

00:18:16.740 --> 00:18:17.790
so the effect's not as huge,

00:18:17.790 --> 00:18:20.220
but there are a few special
races during the season

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:23.220
that are built into the city streets,

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:25.770
which are way cooler, in my opinion,

00:18:25.770 --> 00:18:28.140
even if they present a number
of very unique challenges.

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:31.530
And then the winning, or
even just participating,

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:35.370
is a huge marketing win
for all of these companies.

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:38.040
Obviously, if you have a team dedicated

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:39.960
to the highest end of motorsport

00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:44.040
pushing the envelope with
innovation and engineering,

00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:45.720
the ad copy basically writes itself.

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:48.210
So thanks again to
Bitdefender and Team Ferrari

00:18:48.210 --> 00:18:50.280
for helping make this video possible.

00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:52.440
I got to learn a lot about them too.

00:18:52.440 --> 00:18:54.570
And actually the fact that
Bitdefender technology

00:18:54.570 --> 00:18:56.250
is actually used by Ferrari

00:18:56.250 --> 00:18:58.350
to protect their top-secret innovations.

00:18:58.350 --> 00:19:02.400
But now I'm a fan, I'm
gonna be watching F1.

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:03.900
I hope you enjoyed this video.

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:05.430
My goal for this whole thing was

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:08.280
to hopefully have you be
able to watch a video,

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:09.390
watch an F1 race,

00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:11.670
and not necessarily know
everything that's going on,

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:14.700
but to be able to better
appreciate the race

00:19:14.700 --> 00:19:17.100
and the engineering and the spectacle

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:19.214
of what might happen next.

00:19:19.214 --> 00:19:20.250
(mellow music)

00:19:20.250 --> 00:19:23.280
Thanks for watching. Subscribe for more.

00:19:23.280 --> 00:19:25.023
Catch you the next one. Peace.

